<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Baro-Bhuyans</h1><p class="st-meta">1576 CE – 1611 CE (approx.) · capital Sonargaon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederacy of Bengali landholders led by Isa Khan that resisted the Mughal conquest of Bengal for a generation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daud-khan-karrani">Daud Khan Karrani</a> in 1576 and ended Afghan rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, many landholders in the eastern river delta refused to submit. Known as the Baro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuyan">Bhuyans</a>, the twelve landlords, they gathered around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-khan">Isa Khan</a>, a chief of Afghan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> descent based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonargaon">Sonargaon</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuyan">Bhuyans</a> were Muslim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> zamindars who controlled the waterways of Bhati, the low delta, with fleets of war boats and fortified river posts. They kept their own revenues and alliances, cooperating against Mughal armies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-khan">Isa Khan</a> defeated a Mughal fleet in 1584 and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-i">Man Singh</a>, the Mughal governor.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isa-khan">Isa Khan's</a> death in 1599 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-khan-of-bengal">Musa Khan</a> led the resistance. The Mughal governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> Khan moved the capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> and defeated Musa Khan by 1611, absorbing eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 1576 CE – 1611 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.06%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1576 CE – 1611 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sonargaon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Isa Khan, Musa Khan, Pratapaditya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bengali, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bara Bhuiyans, Twelve Bhuiyans of Bengal, Isa Khan's confederacy</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1594&amp;focus=baro-bhuyans" aria-label="Baro-Bhuyans on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7096 1306 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Baro-Bhuyans lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7416 1562L7339 1518M7416 1562L7018 1385"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7339 1518h0M7018 1385h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7416 1562h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7416 1562h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sonargaon. <a href="/atlas?year=1594&amp;focus=baro-bhuyans">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
